Hi ,I went through the document, and I agree with the overall direction. Sending a JSON payload from Angular and receiving it with @RequestBody in the backend is the cleaner and standard approach, especially for nested data like signSympObj.A few suggestions from my side:1.For signSympObj, we should avoid using Object and keep it strongly typed as List<CdrCaseIdentifier>. That gives better type safety and reduces runtime issues.2.Since our stack is already Java 17 + Spring Boot 3.x, we can use Java records for request DTOs to keep code cleaner and immutable. For example:public record SaveSignsSymptomsAttrRequest(
    Long caseId,
    Long diseaseId,
    List<CdrCaseIdentifier> signSympObj,
    String fromWhere
) {}Spring Boot 3.x can deserialize JSON into records directly.3.Along with backend changes, we should add JUnit tests to confirm the controller receives/parses payload correctly and the save flow still works as expected.4.On the Angular side, we usually don’t need to set Content-Type: application/json explicitly when posting a plain object, since Angular sets it automatically.5.For very large payloads, we should still keep in mind backend/proxy request size limits and configure them if needed.6.For the duplicate-save issue, I suggest we treat this as a good design improvement, but continue duplicate investigation separately unless we have clear proof this parameter format was the root cause.Overall, the design looks good and aligned with best practices.
